## ESP32 OTA Firmware Updates

There are three main approaches, from simplest to most production-ready:

### 1. Arduino OTA (Development Use)

The simplest option — updates over the local network via mDNS. Good for development, not for production.

```cpp
#include <ArduinoOTA.h>
#include <WiFi.h>

void setup() {
  WiFi.begin("SSID", "password");
  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) delay(500);

  ArduinoOTA.setHostname("my-esp32");
  ArduinoOTA.setPassword("ota-secret");
  ArduinoOTA.begin();
}

void loop() {
  ArduinoOTA.handle();
}
```

### 2. HTTP OTA (Production — Pull Model)

The device pulls a firmware binary from an HTTP server. This is the most common production approach.

```cpp
#include <HTTPUpdate.h>
#include <WiFiClientSecure.h>

void checkForUpdate() {
  WiFiClientSecure client;
  client.setCACert(rootCACert); // pin your server's CA

  // Optional: send current version so the server can decide
  httpUpdate.setAuthorization("Bearer", apiKey);

  t_httpUpdate_return ret = httpUpdate.update(
    client,
    "https://firmware.example.com/esp32/firmware.bin",
    CURRENT_VERSION  // sent as x-ESP32-version header
  );

  switch (ret) {
    case HTTP_UPDATE_FAILED:
      Serial.printf("Update failed: %s\n", httpUpdate.getLastErrorString().c_str());
      break;
    case HTTP_UPDATE_NO_UPDATES:
      Serial.println("Already up to date.");
      break;
    case HTTP_UPDATE_OK:
      Serial.println("Update successful, rebooting...");
      break;
  }
}
```

