### Rollback Protection

ESP-IDF supports **automatic rollback** — critical for production:

```c
// After OTA boot, validate the firmware works correctly
// (e.g., can reach the server, sensors read OK, etc.)
esp_ota_mark_app_valid_cancel_rollback();

// If you don't call this within a timeout, the bootloader
// rolls back to the previous partition on next reboot.
```

Enable in `sdkconfig`:
```
CONFIG_BOOTLOADER_APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE=y
```

### Version Management

Implement a version check endpoint so devices don't re-download the same firmware:

```
GET /api/firmware/latest?device=esp32&current=1.2.3
→ 200 { "version": "1.3.0", "url": "...", "sha256": "..." }
→ 304 (no update needed)
```

---

## Production Checklist

| Concern | Recommendation |
|---|---|
| **Transport** | Always use HTTPS with certificate pinning |
| **Authentication** | Device API key or client certificates |
| **Integrity** | Verify SHA-256 hash of the binary before committing |
| **Signing** | Use [Secure Boot v2](https://docs.espressif.com/projects/esp-idf/en/latest/esp32/security/secure-boot-v2.html) to reject unsigned firmware |
| **Rollback** | Enable `APP_ROLLBACK_ENABLE`; mark valid only after self-test |
| **Scheduling** | Check for updates on a jittered interval (avoid thundering herd) |
| **Progress** | Use callbacks to show progress on display/LEDs |
| **Power safety** | OTA is atomic at the partition level — partial writes won't brick |
| **Delta updates** | Consider [esp-delta-ota](https://github.com/espressif/esp-delta-ota) to reduce bandwidth |

---

## Which Approach to Use?

- **Hobbyist / dev bench** → ArduinoOTA
- **Product shipping to customers** → `esp_https_ota` with rollback + secure boot
- **Fleet of devices in the field** → HTTP pull model with a version API, or use a managed platform like [ESP RainMaker](https://rainmaker.espressif.com/) or [Golioth](https://golioth.io/)

